.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name with the companion type
variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred macro,
then creates a map whose keys are ordered by custom_comparison_proc
instead of the default order for key_t. The handle also gets the cleanup
attribute, which destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing scope,
an early return included.

That cleanup attribute is the only difference from
cbmap_construct_cc(3). cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro with
the default order, cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) takes an allocator where
this one takes a comparison procedure, and cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3)
takes both.

Use this scoped variant for a struct key_t, because a key type that the
map doesn't recognize is ordered by default by a raw comparison of the
key's representation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. Every later macro reads a key through
                        it, and the container takes from it the width to
                        expect and the alignment of a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never passes it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and doesn't expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure receives pointers to two keys and returns a negative value
when the first key orders before the second, a positive value when it
orders after it, and zero when the map must treat the two as the same
key. A zero makes the call an update instead of an insert, so the
procedure defines both key equality and key order.

It must give a strict weak order over key_t, and that order must not
change while the map holds elements. With only two bare pointers and no
lengths, the procedure can read no more than the width that key_t fixes,
so the map checks the width of an incoming key against key_t and aligns
its stored keys for key_t.

The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map, which in
this case ends at the closing brace of the enclosing scope.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation
fails. It needs GCC or Clang because of the cleanup attribute, and it uses
the default allocator.

A walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure; cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract.

When the scope exits the map is destroyed and the handle cleared, so an
earlier cbmap_destroy(3) inside the scope is also fine.

The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, and a
function that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], doesn't
compile; the message says to wrap the array in a struct. Keys and values
are copied by assignment and C can't assign an array, so without that
error the map would store the address of the caller's array instead of
its elements. A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Don't return it and don't store it
anywhere that lives longer than the scope, because the cleanup attribute
frees its map when the scope exits and you'd be handing out a handle to
freed memory.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

void summarize(version *v, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(releases, version, int, version_cmp);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(releases, v[i], one);
    }
}                             /* The macro destroys the releases map here */
.fi
